/* News entries for the News page.
   Each entry is one row on the timeline, latest first. Order here doesn't
   matter — entries are sorted by date, newest first, and anything dated ahead
   of today is flagged "Upcoming". Clicking a row (or one of its pictures)
   opens a window with the full image set and write-up.

   Fields:
     id      – optional stable key; falls back to title + position
     date    – YYYY-MM-DD
     title   – headline
     tag     – short label. It rides the split-flap board next to the date and
               picks the entry's accent hue. Known tags: Publication, Preprint,
               Open Data, Dataset, Talk, Event, Award, Press, Fieldwork,
               People, Blog. Anything else still works and gets a colour by
               position. Keep it short — every character is a flap.
     text    – 1–2 sentence teaser shown on the row
     body    – optional array of paragraphs for the window; falls back to `text`
     images  – any number. The row shows them on a flip board of unevenly sized
               tiles — up to four, or three on alternate rows. Tiles keep
               turning over and no two ever show the same picture: with more
               pictures than tiles a tile turns to one waiting off the board,
               and with exactly as many, two tiles trade places instead. One
               or two pictures sit still; five or six give the liveliest
               board. The window shows them all in a gallery.
               Each: { src, alt, credit?: { name, url } }
               A bare string is accepted as shorthand for { src }.
               Entries with no images render as a full-width text row.
     link    – optional { url, label } — or a bare URL string

   A blog post (tag "Blog") is a long read. Its row runs the cover picture
   full width with the headline beneath, and it opens as a page of its own at
   /news/<id> — so give it an id — with the pictures set among the paragraphs.
   It reads these fields differently:
     text    – the standfirst under the headline
     author  – name, or { name, role }. A name matching someone in
               people.jsonc borrows their photo, role and page for the byline.
     cover   – { src, alt } across the top of the post; falls back to the
               first figure
     thumbnail – { src, alt } across the top of the row; falls back to `cover`
     body    – paragraphs and pictures in reading order. A string is a
               paragraph (a "\n" inside it breaks a line; [text](url) makes
               a link). { "figure": {...},
               "caption" } sets one picture; { "figures": [...], "caption" }
               sets a row of them, and a nested array stacks its pictures into
               one column, so [[a, b], [c]] puts a over b beside c. Figures are
               numbered in order, and the reading time is worked out from the
               paragraphs.
     images  – not read; the post's pictures come from `body`
*/
[
  {
    "id": "council-chamber-peoples-emergency-briefing-2026",
    "date": "2026-11-06",
    "title": "BIOLOOM Speaks Up in the Council Chamber: Discussing Nature-related Risks and Socio-Ecological Resilience",
    "tag": "Blog",
    "author": "Kristian George Suszczenia",
    "text": "BIOLOOM joins an expert panel in the Tower Hamlets council chamber, after the first screening of the People’s Emergency Briefing to the local government.",
    "body": [
      {
        "figure": {
          "src": "/news/06-11-2026/th-council-panel.webp",
          "alt": "Six panellists seated in a curve of chairs on the wooden floor of the Tower Hamlets council chamber, one speaking into a microphone, beside a screen titled ‘People’s Emergency Briefing: Panel discussion’, with audience members in the foreground. A Green Week 2026 frame of a green city skyline runs along the bottom of the picture"
        },
        "caption": "The panel discussion in the Tower Hamlets council chamber, following the screening of the People’s Emergency Briefing."
      },
      "This October, BIOLOOM was represented in the local council chamber of Tower Hamlets as I was invited to take part in an expert panel following the first screening of the People’s Emergency Briefing to our university’s local government.",
      "As a brief introduction, this kind of event, which is now reaching 5,000 grass-roots screenings being shown in churches, councils, and backyards alike, stemmed from the UK’s National Emergency Briefing in late 2025. That original event was a briefing to >1,200 MPs, business and faith leaders in Westminster. It represents, in my view, an unprecedented historic milestone in which some of the UK’s top scientists, forecasters and military professionals –experts on risk, climate, economy, nature breakdown, and national security– outlined in no uncertain terms that the risks to our nation (as with others) are escalating with too little response. The People’s Emergency Briefing is the accessible film and discussion version of this event that is taking the UK (and now other countries) by storm. This has coincided with a historic drought year for England, along with reports from top insurers, warnings from a consortium of farmers and grocers, assessments of MI5 and 6, and other arms of the UK government driving toward the same conclusion. The accelerating and un-countered risk of collapsing food supply, water supply, social order, insurance, economy, and way of life is hitting centre stage.",
      "Including ecology’s own highly influential Oxford professor Nathalie Seddon (and one of the more fun of my old lecturers) stating clearly that the nature crisis risks we are facing could eliminate the possibility of the economy at all, as it is currently understood. The Briefing phenomenon is now being replicated in ten other countries, whose own top experts are coming together to issue the same warnings. And I imagine that this number will only grow.",
      "I don’t take it lightly, and feel more responsibility than honour to speak in the council chamber on such a topic. Alongside other panellist experts in infrastructure resilience (Juan Sebastián Cañavera Herrera), water systems (Megan Monkman), and environmental psychology (Marta Saniewska); the discussion was incredible. The kind of discussion that only could have happened on such a stage in 2026. And we brought a distinctly BIOLOOM perspective to the conversation.",
      "Our lab group is all about emphasising the interconnections between humans and nature, and my PhD research in particular confronts the consequences of ecological breakdown on people’s lives and society at large; socio-ecological breakdown. Including how nature loss can compromise our food, medicine, materials, and much more.",
      "So, BIOLOOM brought to the council chamber a reminder that this predicament is not a climate crisis, but a polycrisis of interacting factors that spans from socio- to ecological. From soil depletion, to democratic slide, to biodiversity loss, to fraying social cohesion, perceptions of nature, and trust. There was an enormously positive reception to this thinking during the panel and in discussions afterward with government workers in the audience. We then spoke about food security for a central London borough; even more prescient now as London’s reservoirs are still under projected filling rates. Emphasising the importance of decentralised food production, restoration of integrated agroecological landscapes that form soil rather than deplete it, and prioritising ‘polysolutions’ to a polycrisis.",
      "For sure, the mood has shifted: every question in the chamber was about food, water and safety rather than GDP. We discussed how the housing development of a greenspace in our very-green-limited home may well have been viable in previous decades, but is simply not strategic in this new era where our actions, perceptions, and values must expand into the wider systems that our predecessors have been neglecting. I introduced a reminder that we are truly living in a socio-ecological system, not a stand-alone society, which requires totally different management techniques, priorities and perhaps totally different frames of mind. Co-panellist, Juan, captured and furthered the concept: “We are also living currently a morality crisis. We need to review our values and the way we interrelate with other humans and non-humans.”",
      "Undoubtedly, my lifetime will be a period of enormous and difficult to predict change – both positive and negative. I feel it is important, and increasingly mainstream, to say that. One thing I feel I can predict is that centralising how interwoven our lives are with the human and ecological world around us (and then acting accordingly) will be an inevitable part of our future, as well as an incredible tool for managing these risks and upholding good lives. And that’s what BIOLOOM is all about.",
      "It was an amazing space, full of so much concern and yet so much hope. And, to me, most energising was the newness of the atmosphere. Discussions about catastrophic risks to human way of life that I could never have had before, being spoken through stubborn smiles and kind eyes. In a council chamber no less. Despite such a sombre topic, I could not help but to feel ecstatic on the tube ride home. Something is shifting this year, minds are changing – change is on its way.",
      "Until next time,\nKristian."
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "fabric-of-life-tower-hamlets-summer-fair-2026",
    "date": "2026-09-22",
    "title": "BIOLOOM brings the Fabric of Life to the local community",
    "tag": "Blog",
    "author": "Kristian George Suszczenia",
    "text": "Hands-on crafts to bring together artists, children, mycelial textiles, and socio-ecological systems change.",
    "cover": {
      "src": "/news/22-09-2026/fold-5-installation-detail.webp",
      "alt": "Close-up of the Fabric of Life installation: strips of leaf-matrix paper studded with seeds and pressed plants, woven with mycelium textile and a tuft of grey fur"
    },
    "thumbnail": {
      "src": "/news/22-09-2026/fold-1-stall.webp",
      "alt": "The stall at the fair: a hand holds up a small woven Fabric of Life diagram of coloured ribbons above a table of glass voting jars, bowls of glass beads and squares of handmade leaf paper"
    },
    "body": [
      {
        "figure": {
          "src": "/news/22-09-2026/fold-1-stall.webp",
          "alt": "The stall at the fair: a hand holds up a small woven Fabric of Life diagram of coloured ribbons above a table of glass voting jars, bowls of glass beads and squares of handmade leaf paper"
        },
        "caption": "A look at our stall: the fabric of life diagram, ecological crafts, and voting jars."
      },
      "Have you ever looked at the wooden furniture in your house or the food on your plate and wondered: “How did this get here?” It can be a mind-bender alone to think about all the human hands involved, but what if we go one step further and think about what had to go right in the ecosystem for it to get here? How large the fungal mycelial networks were? How many pollinating species, how many disease-protecting species, how many soil-forming species, how many species providing redundancy, backups for when heatwaves or wildfires or floods take away other crucial cogs? Just for me to sit at this desk, or enjoy this spag bol.",
      "I struggled through this dizzying array of questions, which became a life-changing journey, when I started my PhD researching “Socio-Ecological Systems” in [BIOLOOM Labs](https://bioloom-labs.com/). Socio-ecological Systems are just what they sound like: tossing away the illusory line we draw between people and nature and considering this strange world, not as a society that sits alongside an ‘out there’ nature that it gets resources from, but as a profoundly complex single system. A single system which is mistreating itself.",
      "It would be wrong to write about the amazing community and family engagement event that follows without a touch of the sobering context that drives this work. Most of us are taking part in a certain section of that socio-ecological system that is still figuring itself out. Still trying to find a non-destructive relationship with the non-human parts of the system that support us. All while we are watching those critical foundations of our lives suffer ecological degradation abroad and increasingly at home. In all of this, there is a growing chorus of academics, UN bodies, and front-lining communities concluding that it is precisely our tendency to draw that line, to separate ourselves from the intricate web of life around us, that is causing us to misperceive and therefore mismanage our world. That we need a significant shift in “[cultural narratives and social norms around production and consumption](https://www.ipbes.net/transformative-change-assessment)”. That recognising our place within this single system alongside our non-human partners, and acting like it, may be a critical leverage point for a safer and more just future for us all. What leading ecologist Sandra Diaz promotes as the transition to a “Fabric of Life” worldview.",
      "I wanted to take my complex and unintuitive research, where I simulate networks depicting socio-ecological systems, and make it accessible in the belief that anyone of any age could engage with the world from this ‘Fabric of Life’ perspective if only they were given the chance. And what better way to do so than working with artists and park rangers?",
      "On a heatwave-hot Saturday morning, we set up our stall at the [Friends of Tower Hamlets Cemetery Park](https://fothcp.org/) summer fair, a few minutes down the road from Queen Mary’s main campus, and let the children do it for themselves. But in fact, we had participants of all ages come to get their hands on the ecosystem and weave their very own Fabric of Life Diagram. In a Fabric of Life Diagram, such as the ‘chicken curry’ diagram shown below, we take three steps to go from human-only thinking to Fabric of Life thinking.",
      {
        "figure": {
          "src": "/news/22-09-2026/fold-2-chicken-curry.webp",
          "alt": "A Fabric of Life Diagram of woven satin ribbons, each drawn with a species, beside its back, which reads ‘chicken curry, fabric of life’ beneath a blurred name"
        },
        "caption": "A Fabric of Life Diagram made by a participant showing the direct providers (garlic, tomato, coriander, chicken) and their supporters (songbirds, bees, beetles, and grasses) that are needed for a chicken curry."
      },
      "First, we choose something we value in our lives; in this case, a chicken curry. Then, we think about the species that help directly provide us with this thing we love; here, garlic, tomato, coriander, and chicken. And place them as vertical ribbons, like the warp. Already, we are starting to name and thank the natural elements that prop up our lives, but we go one step further. Now, we think about the species that those species themselves rely upon – which make up the weft threading from left to right. Importantly, we weave this ribbon under a directly providing species if it does not support it, and over if it does. For example, in the ‘chicken curry’ Fabric of Life Diagram: the songbird (blue) helps disperse the seeds of the tomato (red), the bee (pale) pollinates the coriander (green), the beetle (brown) forms soil that all three plants in the curry rely upon, and the wild grass (light green) is needed for the chickens to eat.",
      "The wonderful thing is that every participant goes home with a totally unique, colourful, and vividly interwoven picture of the way our lives are upheld by the complex and caring cat’s cradle of nature – which they can stick up on the fridge. We saw the kaleidoscopic fabric that emerges from thinking about the species that give us our chocolate bar, an ice cream, a sweater, a cup of tea, coffee, and medicinal remedies. At one point, some participants got super creative and made a Fabric of Life Diagram depicting the complex web of nature that was needed to give us the Lord of the Rings film franchise… including the grasses that make the rolling hills of New Zealand such a picturesque location and, of course, the overlooked earthworms that underpin them!",
      {
        "figures": [
          [
            {
              "src": "/news/22-09-2026/fold-3a-coffee.webp",
              "alt": "A woven ribbon diagram for coffee, with ‘Coffee’ written on the felt backing"
            },
            {
              "src": "/news/22-09-2026/fold-3b-ice-cream.webp",
              "alt": "A woven ribbon diagram for ice cream in a tub, with ‘I like nachure so much’ written on the back"
            }
          ],
          {
            "src": "/news/22-09-2026/fold-3c-lord-of-the-rings.webp",
            "alt": "A woven ribbon diagram for the Lord of the Rings films, its ribbons drawn with grasses, an earthworm and an apple"
          }
        ],
        "caption": "A selection of Fabric of Life Diagrams made by participants: coffee (top left), ice cream in a paper tub (bottom left), and the film ‘Lord of the Rings’ (right)."
      },
      "To fully explore the concept, materials artist Qi Shi, who partnered with me throughout this project, took the lead. We developed a Fabric of Life for a chocolate bar with more than 40 kinds of critters and plants (and many more species implied) performing the diverse roles needed to get that tasty snack to us. Including but not limited to: how tiny flies pollinate cocoa trees, how birds of prey are known to decrease pest pressure in sugar cane farms, how tiny fungi live between the tissues of Hazelnut trees and boost their immune system, how mosses growing on trees increase pollination rates, how marsupials, rats, bats and birds disperse seeds, how ladybirds control aphid populations and how millipedes accelerate compost forming under soybean crops. Qi took all the species involved in this great milieu and constructed ribbons that reflected the colours and textures. Such as paper made from fallen leaves to represent the plants, leather grown from living mycelium for the fungi, and of course feathers and fluff for our closer cousins. The result was a five-foot Fabric of Life Diagram, mounted on a ‘floating’ transparent background, that is as messy, complicated, and beautiful as the ecosystem it attempts to illustrate. An eye-catching centrepiece of our stall, as well as an invitation for children to get textural and “touch the ecosystem that underpins society” (in this case with society reduced to a chocolate bar… but the point stands).",
      {
        "figures": [
          {
            "src": "/news/22-09-2026/fold-4-installation.webp",
            "alt": "The five-foot Fabric of Life installation hanging on a clear panel between two trees, woven from strips of leaf paper, mycelium leather, feathers and fur"
          },
          {
            "src": "/news/22-09-2026/fold-5-installation-detail.webp",
            "alt": "Close-up of the installation: strips of leaf-matrix paper studded with seeds and pressed plants, woven with mycelium textile and a tuft of grey fur"
          }
        ],
        "caption": "Our five-foot floating 3D art installation, ‘The Fabric of Life Behind a Chocolate Bar’ (left), and a close-up highlighting the use of ecological materials to make leaf-matrix paper and mycelium-grown textiles (right)."
      },
      "Participants told us they were “shocked” by how the exercise and artwork made them consider how wide the reach and reliance of our lives really extend. But we also wanted to know if it helped them connect more to the unseen ecosystem behind our lives, so we asked – having done this – how does it make you think we should treat nature? Children and parents alike responded: “with kindness”, “with respect”, “like a valued community member”, “like a best friend (that keeps you alive)”, and “as an equal”. But what really hit home for me was being told that, because it is a part of us and we are a part of it, we should simply see looking after nature as “looking after ourselves”. All this wonderful feedback struck at a secret goal of mine – a little test.",
      "I wanted to see if pragmatically thinking about the species we rely on, tracing reliance back through multiple layers of ecosystem (in this case two layers), could act as an accessible stepping stone to the real message hiding within. That being: the sudden realisation that the bee which pollinates the coriander in our curry has its own fabric of life just like us, and each of those species relies on its own fabric too, and so on and so on. That, if we were to truly try and weave the Fabric of Life showing the species which gift us even a simple curry or a single chocolate bar, it would be of incomprehensible size and complexity. And may well include most of the species on Earth… The secret message hidden within an exercise that seems on the surface about picking and choosing species is, in fact, that such an effort is impossible. That nature, as a whole, needs our reciprocity – for both its health and our own. From the bright smiles and kind words we received, we believe that the Fabric of Life Diagram proved that point. Children did not leave thinking that the four supporting species they happened to choose were important to their lives. No, children are far better at systems thinking than we give them credit for. They left with a personal sense that the fabric of life is far greater than our little craft, that nature really underpins so much more, and with a lasting memory of gratitude that they built with their own two hands. Gratitude for this wonderful ecosystem, and the wonderful life it gifts to us.",
      {
        "figure": {
          "src": "/news/22-09-2026/fold-6-kristian-and-qi.webp",
          "alt": "Kristian and Qi sitting at the Queen Mary stall under a gazebo at the summer fair, the installation standing on a wooden easel beside them"
        },
        "caption": "Kristian and Qi at the Friends of Tower Hamlets Cemetery Park Summer Fair."
      },
      "Ultimately, we hope to give participants a sense of reciprocity to, and kinship with, nature that is derived from getting stuck in, personal connection, and meeting the participant at their current worldview (which may well be a very human-centred and use-value oriented) – more than just lectures or words. Perhaps this will be one of the ways we can help turn this ship, our home, around.",
      "If you think so too, or are interested in exploring this topic, please do reach out so that we can share guidance to reproduce the event, collaborate on future projects, or establish research connections.",
      "Until next time, with hope,\nKristian."
    ]
  },
